  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nA broad area of high pressure is centered over the eastern Great \nLakes early this morning, resulting in a light easterly wind and dry \nconditions for the region. In the upper-levels, broad upper\nridging is noted with a 500mb ridge over the Southwest. This \nwill help keep the next shortwave, moving southeast out Alberta\nand into the Northern Plains, to stay north of the area. It \nwill however, push another weak front south through the area\nSunday, reinforcing the drier airmass over the region. \nRegarding temperatures this weekend, highs today will be similar\nto slightly warmer than Friday, with highs in the low to mid \n70s expected, which is very close to normal for this time of \nyear. Similar near-normal to slightly above-normal conditions \nare expected Sunday. For next week, low-level flow turns \nsoutherly by Tuesday, as the surface high shifts into the \neastern US. This southerly flow will help temperatures climb \nback above normal by Tuesday with highs climbing to 10-15 \ndegrees above normal values for the later half of next week. \n\nWhile several weak shortwaves will move across the middle of the \ncountry, there is a lack of moisture available for any precipitation \nto occur. As a result, the entire forecast looks dry with a\ngreater than 95% of dry conditions. The only day when there is \nmore than a 0-5% chance is Thursday night. A shortwave moves \neast, mainly north of the area, with some minor ascent noted \nalong the northern edge of the low-level jet. For now, the \natmosphere looks too dry to carry anything more than around a 5%\nchance of precipitation across northern Missouri.",
